# ZIGNOS base system — the canonical package set.
#
# Gentoo-style minimal: boot, a working toolchain and the package manager.
# Everything else (editors, SSH, network clients, WiFi, docs, extra
# filesystems) is installed by the admin with "zpkg install <pkg>".
#
# This file is the single definition of "what a base ZIGNOS system is".
# build-system.sh applies it (zpkg skips what is already installed, so it
# is idempotent — run it after every recipe-tree update to roll forward).
# Dependencies and makedepends of these packages are installed
# automatically, so the full base is larger than this list.
#
# One package name per line; '#' starts a comment. Order does not matter
# (zpkg resolves dependencies), sections are just for reading.

# toolchain and libc
glibc
gcc
binutils
make
linux-headers

# shell and the POSIX tool set recipes build with
bash
coreutils
sed
grep
gawk
diffutils
findutils
tar
gzip
xz
bzip2
m4
patch
file
pkgconf

# init, device nodes and the login path
busybox
openrc
initscripts
util-linux
shadow
e2fsprogs
kbd

# kernel and bootloader (kmod and initramfs come in through linux)
linux
grub

# package manager and TLS trust for source downloads
zpkg
ca-certificates

# mkfs.vfat for the UEFI system partition (used by zignos-install)
dosfstools

# Deliberately NOT in the base set (all installable with zpkg):
#   openssh dhcpcd wpa_supplicant dbus chrony
#       remote access, network configuration, time sync. Install them and
#       enable the service yourself (rc-update add ...).
#   openssl perl python3 cmake meson ninja go
#       build dependencies; zpkg pulls them in when a recipe needs them
#       (go is already here indirectly: zpkg depends on it).
#   vim nano less man-db man-pages
#       editors and documentation.
#   git curl wget rsync tmux htop which
#       everyday tools (main/).
#   doas sudo
#       privilege escalation (su is available from util-linux/shadow).
#   efibootmgr efivar
#       UEFI boot-entry management. zignos-install boots through the
#       removable \EFI\BOOT\BOOTX64.EFI path; install efibootmgr when you
#       want NVRAM entries (the installer registers one when it is present).
